          {
            "kind": "heading",
            "level": 2,
            "text": "1. How print spread",
            "anchor": "1. How print spread"
          },
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "text": "Within fifty years of the [Gutenberg Bible](arestoteles://note/Gutenberg%20Bible), presses ran in more than 250 European towns (Druckorte). Print followed trade routes, not borders."
          },
          {
            "kind": "section_link",
            "note": "Spread of print"
          },
          {
            "kind": "heading",
            "level": 2,
            "text": "2. Print and the Reformation",
            "anchor": "2. Print and the Reformation"
          },
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "text": "Luther's pamphlets were cheap, short and in German, and printers reprinted whatever sold: see [Information wants to be cheap](arestoteles://note/Information%20wants%20to%20be%20cheap) and [Did print cause the Reformation](arestoteles://note/Did%20print%20cause%20the%20Reformation)."
          },
          {
            "kind": "callout",
            "style": "claude",
            "title": "A rival view",
            "text": "Elizabeth Eisenstein (*The Printing Press as an Agent of Change*, 1979) gives the press the leading role; Andrew Pettegree argues that pamphlets mattered more than books.",
            "index": 0
          },
          {
            "kind": "heading",
            "level": 2,
            "text": "How my view changed",
            "anchor": "How my view changed"
          },
          {
            "kind": "list",
            "ordered": false,
            "items": [
              {
                "text": "**2026-10-20, position 1:** I thought: Print caused the Reformation. What changed it: Earlier reformers failed without a press, but the press alone convinced nobody.",
                "depth": 0
              }
            ]
          },
